\details{
In \code{\link[=mascot]{mascot()}} and its helpers, \code{NULL} (where accepted) means none or the
default; in \code{\link[=update_mascot]{update_mascot()}} it means unchanged. An empty helper
(\code{mascot_bubble()}, \code{mascot_status()}, \code{mascot_theme()}, \code{mascot_action()} or
\code{mascot_popup_menu()}) resets that setting.

\code{\link[=mascot]{mascot()}} and the helpers raise an error on an invalid value;
\code{\link[=update_mascot]{update_mascot()}} warns instead and leaves the mascot unchanged. See
\link{mascot-package} for condition classes.

Use the always visible status line for a short description of the current state.
Use a bubble for a message about something that happened; it may time out.
Use \code{mascot_bubble(..., duration = Inf)} for a message the user must act on,
such as an invalid form, because a timed message can vanish before it is read.

When minimised, the status remains available to screen readers. See
\code{\link[=mascot_placement]{mascot_placement()}} for restore controls and message timing.

In Shiny the mascot is also an input. \verb{input$<id>} only tells the server that
the mascot has appeared; its contents are internal, so do not rely on them.

\strong{In modules, \code{golem} and rhino apps.} Use the module's \code{ns()} in the UI and
its local id in the server. \code{session$ns("helper")} also works in the server;
the namespace is not added twice. In nested modules, namespace the child module's
id in the parent UI and call its server with the local child id. Inputs such
as \code{input$helper_menu}, \code{input$helper_click}, \code{input$helper_position} and
\code{input$helper_minimised} belong to that child module.
A module can put the mascot in its UI and update it from its server:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{helper_ui <- function(id) \{
  ns <- shiny::NS(id)
  mascot(ns("helper"))
\}
helper_server <- function(id) \{
  shiny::moduleServer(id, function(input, output, session) \{
    update_mascot("helper", mood = "happy")
  \})
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The same pattern works in the function-built \code{app_ui()} of a \code{golem} app and in
rhino's \verb{app/view/} modules using \code{\link[shiny:moduleServer]{shiny::moduleServer()}}. The package's
scripts and styles are attached through \code{\link[htmltools:htmlDependency]{htmltools::htmlDependency()}},
including with \code{\link[shiny:renderUI]{shiny::renderUI()}}, and shared by mascots on the page.
A built-in character's data travels the same way, once per page for each
character in use; a custom character's data stays inside its own tag.
You do not need to copy any of the package's files into your app. Try
\code{mascot_example("golem")} (requires \code{golem}) or \code{mascot_example("rhino")}
(a plain Shiny structure without rhino or box).

\strong{Showing the mascot later.} To add the mascot only when it is needed, build
it in the server with \code{\link[shiny:renderUI]{shiny::renderUI()}} and place \code{\link[shiny:uiOutput]{shiny::uiOutput()}} in the
UI. Updates sent before it appears are kept until it does, so the first
\code{update_mascot()} can run in the same observer:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{library(shiny)
library(mascot)

ui <- fluidPage(
  actionButton("start", "Start the review"),
  actionButton("finish", "Finish"),
  uiOutput("helper_ui")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) \{
  shown <- reactiveVal(FALSE)
  output$helper_ui <- renderUI(\{
    req(shown())
    mascot("helper", status = "Reviewing")
  \})
  observeEvent(input$start, \{
    shown(TRUE)
    # Sent before the mascot exists: kept until it appears.
    update_mascot("helper", mood = "thinking")
  \})
  observeEvent(input$finish, \{
    update_mascot("helper", mood = "happy", status = "Review complete")
  \})
\}

shinyApp(ui, server)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The \code{reactiveVal()} makes the mascot appear once: clicking \strong{Start the
review} again does not rebuild it, so it keeps its mood and position.

\strong{Clicks.} With \code{click}, the mascot is a button: an action named by its
label, or a menu. Each time the visitor activates the action (a click or tap,
Enter or Space while it has keyboard focus, or a screen reader's activate
command), \verb{input$<id>_click} is set to the number of activations since the
mascot appeared, so \code{shiny::observeEvent(input$helper_click, ...)} runs each
time for an action.
Opening and closing a menu never sets this input. Dragging a floating mascot
never counts as a click. See \code{\link[=mascot_action]{mascot_action()}} and \code{\link[=mascot_popup_menu]{mascot_popup_menu()}}.
}
